# The locations this index covers.
#
# One absolute path per line, '#' comments and blank lines ignored. This is the file
# `esidx build --roots-file=` reads, and it exists because systemd does not split an
# environment file's value into words: a list of locations cannot be one KEY=value, and
# quoting it into one does not survive either.
#
# This file is the whole of the deployment. There is no build command to run, before or
# after an install: the service compares this file with the locations its snapshot covers
# every time it starts -- and every 5 s while it runs -- and whenever the two disagree it
# rebuilds the index in a child process and swaps it in, answering clients from the index
# it has throughout (design D11). A fresh install with no snapshot at all starts with an
# empty index and builds; an edit made while the service was stopped is picked up when it
# comes back; the first build and every later one are the same event. The swap rewrites
# the snapshot on disk, so a restart comes up on the locations that are in it now.
#
# A location may appear at most once, and no location may be inside another. Both are
# refused rather than tolerated: together they would give every file below them two rows,
# and once they are in the index the duplicate is indistinguishable from the original --
# `path:` answers with two ids and `count:` is twice the truth. `esidx build` checks this
# against the filesystem before it walks anything, so a refused build leaves no snapshot.
#
# These are absolute paths in *this* machine's namespace, and they are what the client is
# shown: the paths stored in the index are the ones named here, with no mapping back to
# wherever the data really lives. A bind mount therefore indexes as the mount point, not
# as the directory behind it.
#
# Nothing here is scanned by the package: a full scan is 60-90 s on a real tree, and that
# has no place in `dpkg -i`. It is scanned by the service, as the user the service becomes
# (ESIDX_USER in /etc/default/esidx) -- which is what makes the index a view of what *that*
# identity may read rather than of what root can. So the thing to check when a location
# comes back emptier than expected is that user's permissions on it, not this file.
#
# One hardening note: the unit ships ProtectHome=yes, which makes /home, /root and
# /run/user empty inside the service. A location under any of them therefore indexes as
# nothing unless the unit is told otherwise:
#
#   systemctl edit esidx      #  [Service]
#                             #  ProtectHome=no
/work
